{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791130444,"nonce":"Sx22joGLQS9P8LFeUM3e6nOa2z0=","message":"Regression in deeeec98, reported by the ashui session and fixed.\n\nThe pass treated every child of a baseline-aligned flex container as a\nparticipant. An absolutely positioned child is not a flex item: CSS\npositions it against the container's padding box and it takes no part in\nalignment. Shifting one moves a box whose position its author already\ncomputed, which is exactly what an inline-flow implementation does when\nit places each piece itself. ashui lays out inline text that way, with\neach piece position: absolute inside a row flex container that carries\nalign-items: baseline, and saw its inline code land 1px off.\n\nTwo paths needed it, both as reported:\n\n- aligns_to_baseline now excludes out-of-flow children, so they are\n  never shifted.\n- baseline_offset's container walk skips them too, so a floating child\n  cannot define the container's first baseline. That one was worth 53px\n  in the test, not 1px.\n\nBoth are covered, and both tests were checked against a build with the\nguard neutralised so they cannot pass vacuously. The first version of\nthe shift test did pass vacuously: the absolute child sat lower than the\nin-flow item, which made it the deepest baseline and so never the one\nmoved. Moving it shallower gave the test its teeth.","files":null}]}